, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." 13194139536111 true] [17592186384154 1034 "e^{-2t}" 13194139536111 true] [17592186384154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536111 true] [17592186384154 1111 #uuid "1a78e03c-16e4-51ea-9f0c-daa8b8eb2f0e" 13194139536111 true] [17592186384155 1034 "1" 13194139536111 true] [17592186384155 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536111 true] [17592186384155 1111 #uuid "b132061d-afd4-5ef8-8d19-59a0a21b84a5" 13194139536111 true] [17592186384156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384157 13194139536111 true] [17592186384156 1089 "q-332591" 13194139536111 true] [17592186384156 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536111 true] [17592186384156 1100 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" 13194139536111 true] [17592186384156 1113 #uuid "cdbef393-ee24-56cd-abfd-6178ddb5b54d" 13194139536111 true] [17592186384156 1145 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system o